      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Viral disease of dogs (Canidae) caused by canine adenovirus-1 (CAV-1). It is serologically homogeneous and antigenically distinct from respiratory CAV-2, the causative agent of canine infectious laryngotracheitis.",
              "pdf_page": 710,
              "printed_page": 628,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "CAV-1 has worldwide geographic distribution and it also causes disease in wolves, coyotes, skunks, and bears. It causes encephalitis in foxes.",
              "pdf_page": 710,
              "printed_page": 628,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Virus is spread via direct dog-to-dog oronasal contact or via contact of contaminated fomites. Airborne transmission is not an important means of contracting the virus.",
              "pdf_page": 710,
              "printed_page": 628,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Virus initially replicates in tonsils, then spreads to regional lymph nodes and bloodstream via lymphatics.",
              "pdf_page": 710,
              "printed_page": 628,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Infection—targets parenchymal organs (especially liver), eyes, endothelium, lung, spleen, kidneys, and brain.",
              "pdf_page": 710,
              "printed_page": 628,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Oronasal exposure—viremia (4–8 days); virus shed in saliva and feces; initial dispersal to hepatic macrophages (hepatic Kupffer cells) and endothelium; replicates in Kupffer cells; damages adjacent hepatocytes producing massive viremia when released.",
              "pdf_page": 710,
              "printed_page": 628,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Adequate antibody response clears organs in 10–14 days; virus can persist in renal tubules, glomeruli, iris, ciliary body, and cornea. Virus can be shed in urine for 6–9 months.",
              "pdf_page": 710,
              "printed_page": 628,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Chronic hepatitis—after infection in dogs with only partial neutralizing antibody response.",
              "pdf_page": 710,
              "printed_page": 628,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cytotoxic ocular injury—anterior uveitis; leads to classic “hepatitis blue eye” and can cause glaucoma. Develops in ~1% of dogs after modified live virus (MLV) vaccine.",
              "pdf_page": 710,
              "printed_page": 628,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Glomerulonephritis can develop 1–2 weeks after acute signs resolve and produce proteinuria and interstitial nephritis; chronic renal failure has not been described.",
              "pdf_page": 710,
              "printed_page": 628,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
